# What the composer says each part's regime is, beside what that part's own filing declares. One
# row per resolved part carrying a regime handle, with both claims and whether they agree.
#
# Two claims the grammar cannot compare
#   Two parties, two claims about one thing, and the grammar cannot ask whether they agree.
#   `asrt:part/asrt:layer/asrt:regime` is a handle into the composition's own declarations, and
#   XSD 1.0 checks that it resolves: `compositionRegimeId` is the key, `partRegime` the keyref.
#   That is the strongest thing a grammar can do here and it stops at the document edge, because an
#   identity constraint is scoped to one document and the schema's own annotation says so. Whether
#   the composer's claim matches what the other filing declares about itself is exactly what falls
#   to a rule.
#
# The framework is compared as a pair, never as a code
#   `pm:Regime`'s annotation is emphatic: SAF-T PT publishes `S · M · N · O`, IES AnexoASNC
#   publishes `NIC · NCRF · NCRF-PE · NC-ME`, the lists do not line up, and "a code without its
#   authority is genuinely ambiguous rather than merely unattributed". So the join is on taxonomy
#   and value. Comparing values alone would call `NCRF-PE` and `S` different frameworks and `M`
#   and `M` the same one, both wrongly.
#
# Agreement is membership, not equality
#   A part filing may declare more than one regime, and `refutation` does, so the composer's
#   framework must appear among the frameworks that filing declares. Equality would accuse a
#   document for declaring a second authority's coding, which is the very thing the tall `regime`
#   table exists to permit.
#
# `agrees` is NULL where either side is typed absent
#   Not false. A fixture reporting under no framework and a composer who said `notApplicable` have
#   not disagreed; the question does not arise. `checks/part_regime_disagrees.sqlc` keeps that out
#   of its population rather than reading it as a pass.
-- composition/parts.sqlc's regime handle resolved into composition/composer_regimes.sqlc, against
-- the part filing's own composition/regimes.sqlc.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, p.part_regime,
       cr.framework_taxonomy AS composer_taxonomy,
       cr.framework_value    AS composer_value,
       cr.framework_absent   AS composer_absent,
       (cr.framework_value IS NOT NULL AND EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM (
                :compose(composition/regimes.sqlc)
            ) r
            WHERE r.filing = p.part_filing
              AND r.framework_taxonomy = cr.framework_taxonomy
              AND r.framework_value    = cr.framework_value))            AS agrees,
       (SELECT count(*)
        FROM (
            :compose(composition/regimes.sqlc)
        ) r
        WHERE r.filing = p.part_filing AND r.framework_value IS NOT NULL) AS frameworks_the_filing_states
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/composer_regimes.sqlc)
) cr ON cr.composition = p.composition AND cr.id = p.part_regime
